WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:22.956
Today, clearly,
I am a noble gas.

00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:29.029
Thanks to-- it was
actually the TAs' idea.

00:00:29.029 --> 00:00:31.731
And I was like, of course,
that sounds awesome.

00:00:31.731 --> 00:00:36.870
So I hope you guys have
a fantastic Halloween.

00:00:36.870 --> 00:00:40.306
Obviously, what you want
to do is any kind of trick

00:00:40.306 --> 00:00:43.243
or treating or partying,
you know the drill,

00:00:43.243 --> 00:00:47.113
you bring your periodic
table, because you never know.

00:00:47.113 --> 00:00:48.882
And that's what happened--

00:00:48.882 --> 00:00:53.887
so on Sunday right before
the exam, I got this.

00:00:53.887 --> 00:00:56.623
And so this is
Charles and Raymond.

00:00:56.623 --> 00:00:59.359
And they're saying we
wanted to celebrate the Sox

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:03.496
victory in Boston, but because
the midterm was the next day,

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.899
we brought along our
periodic table to study.

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.634
There were so many
people moving around it

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:10.970
felt like we were in
a sea of electrons.

00:01:10.970 --> 00:01:12.472
And of course, the
only thing that

00:01:12.472 --> 00:01:14.140
could have improved
on that was actually

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.341
like a little dance video.

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:16.509
But that will probably come.

00:01:16.509 --> 00:01:18.678
I'm sure there on that next.

00:01:18.678 --> 00:01:21.414
But you see, they brought it
not just to study for the exam,

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:24.084
because they didn't know if
it was going to be important

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:25.618
or not at the rally.

00:01:25.618 --> 00:01:27.854
You never know.

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:31.558
So make sure you have your
periodic table with you.

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:37.697
Now, OK, oh, yeah, they
mentioned the exam.

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:41.101
So speaking of the exam.

00:01:41.101 --> 00:01:42.502
Here are the results.

00:01:42.502 --> 00:01:47.774
And you can see that there is
a pretty wide distribution.

00:01:47.774 --> 00:01:50.009
You can also see
the averages is 77.

00:01:50.009 --> 00:01:52.812
And just to remind you--
that's about here--

00:01:52.812 --> 00:01:57.684
just to remind you, these
marks, 85, that's the A range.

00:01:57.684 --> 00:01:59.085
This is the B range.

00:01:59.085 --> 00:02:02.288
So the average was in the
solid B range territory.

00:02:03.556 --> 00:02:05.925
And so it goes.

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:10.497
The standard deviation was 12.

00:02:10.497 --> 00:02:16.369
Now I could tell-- you
know, the exam 1,

00:02:16.369 --> 00:02:19.772
the topics of exam 1 many
of you had already seen.

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:22.942
And I think in this
exam, some of you had not

00:02:22.942 --> 00:02:24.477
seen some of these
topics, especially

00:02:24.477 --> 00:02:27.013
like the
crystallography, but also

00:02:27.013 --> 00:02:30.617
even the molecular orbitals,
the band structure.

00:02:30.617 --> 00:02:37.090
And I could tell that, you know,
some people had to work harder,

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:41.394
maybe a little bit stressed,
and I could sense, some of that.

00:02:41.394 --> 00:02:44.564
But when I get
stressed what I do

00:02:44.564 --> 00:02:47.033
is I need to kind of
de-stress somehow.

00:02:47.033 --> 00:02:49.102
And sometimes some
people listen to music,

00:02:49.102 --> 00:02:50.203
some of the other things.

00:02:50.203 --> 00:02:54.908
I always like comedy
as a way of relaxing.

00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:56.543
And so what I do
is I'll Google like

00:02:56.543 --> 00:02:59.012
for articles about Harvard.

00:02:59.012 --> 00:03:04.117
And so I found this one.

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:07.187
This is actually an
article in The Crimson

00:03:07.187 --> 00:03:08.521
from a few years ago.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:11.691
And the most common
grade at Harvard

00:03:11.691 --> 00:03:16.529
is actually an A, a solid
A, the most common grade.

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:22.435
Suspicion that the college
employs a softer grading

00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:24.637
standard than many of
its peer institutions.

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:26.206
You think?

00:03:26.206 --> 00:03:31.277
I mean, if everyone's
getting an A, you think?

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:35.048
So what I wanted to point
out is there's a difference.

00:03:35.048 --> 00:03:38.484
And it's not just about
your privileged, therefore,

00:03:38.484 --> 00:03:42.522
you deserve an A, whereas
we know that that actually

00:03:42.522 --> 00:03:43.790
takes work.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:45.992
That's not the point
I want to make.

00:03:45.992 --> 00:03:47.894
The point I want to make
is that we know also

00:03:47.894 --> 00:03:51.030
what to do when we miss things.

00:03:51.030 --> 00:03:54.067
We know what to do when
we don't get things right.

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:56.536
That's when you do the work.

00:03:56.536 --> 00:03:58.605
So wherever you
are on this curve,

00:03:58.605 --> 00:04:01.140
go back and figure
out what didn't work.

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.141
What did you miss?

00:04:02.141 --> 00:04:05.812
Figure that out, because
that's where you learn.

00:04:05.812 --> 00:04:08.881
Thomas Edison who said,
I never failed, I just

00:04:08.881 --> 00:04:13.453
did 10,000 experiments
that didn't work.

00:04:13.453 --> 00:04:14.754
You've got to get through that.

00:04:14.754 --> 00:04:16.555
You've got to know that
it takes hard work.

00:04:16.555 --> 00:04:18.224
And that's the thing
that we know here.

00:04:18.224 --> 00:04:19.158
That's the difference.

00:04:19.158 --> 00:04:22.328
So please make sure you
do that with exam 2.

00:04:22.328 --> 00:04:25.598
We are all here to help
you continue learning.

00:04:25.598 --> 00:04:26.332
I'm a noble gas.

00:04:30.003 --> 00:04:34.707
And speaking of continuing
to learn, where were we?

00:04:34.707 --> 00:04:35.207
X-rays.

00:04:39.846 --> 00:04:43.549
On Friday, we had sort of
some other things going on.

00:04:43.549 --> 00:04:45.852
But I also was trying to
teach you guys about X-rays.

00:04:49.422 --> 00:04:52.825
And what we did is we learned
how they're generated.

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:53.393
Remember that?

00:04:53.393 --> 00:04:56.963
The Roentgen experiments.

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:59.932
And so I want to pick up
here with the kinds of X-rays

00:04:59.932 --> 00:05:00.533
that we have.

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:01.801
There are two kinds of X-rays.

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:08.408
And we learned about
those on Friday.

00:05:08.408 --> 00:05:10.910
And I want to just
remind you about those.

00:05:10.910 --> 00:05:17.116
And I also want to show you
a video to kind of recapture

00:05:17.116 --> 00:05:19.185
what they are.

00:05:19.185 --> 00:05:24.123
The first kind of
X-ray, remember,

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:26.459
we plotted this as intensity.

00:05:26.459 --> 00:05:31.664
And I'm not going to
draw the cathode ray

00:05:31.664 --> 00:05:34.834
tube again and the experiment
that Roentgen did and all that.

00:05:34.834 --> 00:05:37.270
But I'm going to just jump
to the two kinds of x-rays

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:39.005
that you get.

00:05:39.005 --> 00:05:42.608
And so if you plot
the wavelength

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:46.512
of the X-ray versus the
intensity of the X-ray,

00:05:46.512 --> 00:05:49.582
then one kind is
the Bremsstrahlung,

00:05:49.582 --> 00:05:54.354
which comes from that that
electron getting slowed down.

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:57.357
And if it slows down,
it emits radiation

00:05:57.357 --> 00:05:59.058
in a continuous spectrum.

00:06:02.895 --> 00:06:06.065
And remember, we
sort of drew these.

00:06:09.068 --> 00:06:15.441
And this would be like the
incident electron energy.

00:06:15.441 --> 00:06:21.881
Let's say incident
energy of the electron.

00:06:21.881 --> 00:06:24.617
Maybe like that's, OK, I
don't know, like 10 keV.

00:06:27.653 --> 00:06:30.790
And then up here the incident
energy of the electron

00:06:30.790 --> 00:06:35.161
was you know something
like maybe 30 keV.

00:06:35.161 --> 00:06:40.133
And so you see that as you
hit that anode, remember what

00:06:40.133 --> 00:06:44.003
Roentgen did, he took
a cathode ray tube

00:06:44.003 --> 00:06:45.938
and he upped the voltage.

00:06:45.938 --> 00:06:47.440
So he really cranked
up the voltage.

00:06:47.440 --> 00:06:49.709
So those electrons
coming off the cathode

00:06:49.709 --> 00:06:52.478
are going really fast.

00:06:52.478 --> 00:06:56.249
And then what he did is he put
a piece of metal in their way.

00:06:56.249 --> 00:07:00.019
And what happened is those
really high energy electrons,

00:07:00.019 --> 00:07:03.489
they see those metal
atoms and sometimes they

00:07:03.489 --> 00:07:06.025
get inside the electron
cloud and they turn.

00:07:06.025 --> 00:07:09.996
And that's when they give off
this continuous radiation.

00:07:09.996 --> 00:07:14.066
But we also know that
there is a limit here.

00:07:14.066 --> 00:07:14.867
Remember that?

00:07:14.867 --> 00:07:16.002
We talked about that?

00:07:16.002 --> 00:07:28.247
And that this limit is
set by this maximum.

00:07:28.247 --> 00:07:33.619
It's just the maximum
amount of energy

00:07:33.619 --> 00:07:35.288
that you could get
out of a photon being

00:07:35.288 --> 00:07:38.957
emitted this way would be
equal to the incoming electron.

00:07:38.957 --> 00:07:42.361
The incoming electron
transferred all of its energy

00:07:42.361 --> 00:07:43.362
to the photon.

00:07:43.362 --> 00:07:45.031
So that's why
there is a maximum.

00:07:45.031 --> 00:07:49.635
And it's also why it increases--
remember, shorter wavelength,

00:07:49.635 --> 00:07:51.170
higher energy--

00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:53.973
so it increases as you
increase the energy

00:07:53.973 --> 00:07:55.107
of the instant an electron.

00:07:57.844 --> 00:07:59.278
That is Bremsstrahlung.

00:07:59.278 --> 00:08:01.147
Let's watch a video,
because this is actually

00:08:01.147 --> 00:08:05.284
a very nicely done video that
captures it with animation.

00:08:09.021 --> 00:08:12.024
So OK, here's your CRT.

00:08:12.024 --> 00:08:13.359
Now here they come.

00:08:13.359 --> 00:08:15.061
Those are those electrons.

00:08:15.061 --> 00:08:16.762
And that voltage is high.

00:08:16.762 --> 00:08:17.663
So they're coming out.

00:08:17.663 --> 00:08:20.266
Look at them coming
out really fast.

00:08:20.266 --> 00:08:23.503
Lots of kinetic energy.

00:08:23.503 --> 00:08:25.037
And then they go.

00:08:25.037 --> 00:08:26.973
And then this is
what Roentgen did.

00:08:26.973 --> 00:08:29.108
He put a little piece
of metal in there.

00:08:29.108 --> 00:08:31.677
So now those electrons
are hitting the metal.

00:08:31.677 --> 00:08:33.846
So those are the two things
that he did differently.

00:08:33.846 --> 00:08:37.884
Remember, the room lit up even
when all the lights were off.

00:08:37.884 --> 00:08:38.985
There they are.

00:08:38.985 --> 00:08:39.818
Nice.

00:08:39.818 --> 00:08:40.520
Good electrons.

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:41.587
Oh, and here they go.

00:08:41.587 --> 00:08:44.457
And there's a metal atom.

00:08:44.457 --> 00:08:46.058
And oh look at that.

00:08:46.058 --> 00:08:47.627
Now what are these?

00:08:47.627 --> 00:08:49.328
Those are x-rays coming off.

00:08:49.328 --> 00:08:51.097
Those are the electrons
hitting the metal.

00:08:51.097 --> 00:08:53.232
And here's the metal atom.

00:08:53.232 --> 00:08:55.167
And as the electron
comes in, remember

00:08:55.167 --> 00:08:58.204
it sees the charge
of the nucleus.

00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:00.873
And it gets deflected.

00:09:00.873 --> 00:09:03.509
And that deflection
loses energy.

00:09:03.509 --> 00:09:05.578
And that loss of energy
goes into a photon.

00:09:05.578 --> 00:09:08.381
Now, because these electrons
have such high energy

00:09:08.381 --> 00:09:17.056
to start with, the energies
of the photons are very high.

00:09:17.056 --> 00:09:17.823
They're x-rays.

00:09:21.994 --> 00:09:23.496
And so here's the wavelength.

00:09:23.496 --> 00:09:24.463
And you can see that--

00:09:24.463 --> 00:09:26.799
well, it's sort of a little
hard to see this blue range.

00:09:26.799 --> 00:09:29.068
But see higher energy,
shorter wavelength.

00:09:29.068 --> 00:09:30.870
Lower energy, longer wavelength.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:31.804
Very nice.

00:09:31.804 --> 00:09:35.575
So that's the animation
of Bremsstrahlung.

00:09:35.575 --> 00:09:36.275
OK.

00:09:36.275 --> 00:09:37.209
Good.

00:09:37.209 --> 00:09:39.679
Now the thing is
what we also learned

00:09:39.679 --> 00:09:42.648
is that there is
another type of X-ray.

00:09:42.648 --> 00:09:44.250
There's another type of X-ray.

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:49.255
And in fact, if you crank
this up high enough,

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:50.423
you can get that other type.

00:09:50.423 --> 00:09:51.790
So now, we go higher.

00:09:51.790 --> 00:09:53.626
Oh, we didn't see it here.

00:09:53.626 --> 00:09:55.695
It just looks like
that and that and that.

00:09:55.695 --> 00:10:01.167
And then all of a sudden we go
up to 40 keV and we see this.

00:10:06.439 --> 00:10:07.239
Why?

00:10:07.239 --> 00:10:08.874
What happened here?

00:10:08.874 --> 00:10:11.277
And what happened here is a
totally different mechanism

00:10:11.277 --> 00:10:13.212
for generating X-rays.

00:10:13.212 --> 00:10:15.915
And that was the second type
that we talked about on Friday.

00:10:15.915 --> 00:10:19.919
Those are called characteristic.

00:10:19.919 --> 00:10:25.124
And the reason is that--
remember, we have these levels,

00:10:25.124 --> 00:10:28.327
which now that we are
talking about X-rays,

00:10:28.327 --> 00:10:31.597
we give them
letters, K, L, M, N.

00:10:31.597 --> 00:10:33.532
But it's just the
quantum numbers.

00:10:33.532 --> 00:10:35.167
n equals 1.

00:10:35.167 --> 00:10:38.204
n equals 2.

00:10:38.204 --> 00:10:39.939
n equals 3.

00:10:39.939 --> 00:10:42.408
n equals 4.

00:10:42.408 --> 00:10:44.810
And what we said is that
the weight characteristic

00:10:44.810 --> 00:10:48.914
X-rays are labeled is
that if an electron is

00:10:48.914 --> 00:10:55.588
excited from this lower level,
if an electron is knocked out

00:10:55.588 --> 00:10:58.391
of here, then there's a place
for an electron from here

00:10:58.391 --> 00:11:00.426
to go down.

00:11:00.426 --> 00:11:02.061
Maybe the electron is excited.

00:11:02.061 --> 00:11:03.829
Maybe the electron
is kicked out.

00:11:03.829 --> 00:11:06.532
And then something
here can come down.

00:11:06.532 --> 00:11:13.172
And when that happens, just
like in the Bohr model,

00:11:13.172 --> 00:11:15.741
you get radiation.

00:11:15.741 --> 00:11:19.412
But now, unlike
hydrogen 13.6 electrons,

00:11:19.412 --> 00:11:21.380
these are keV of energy.

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:23.382
These are very high energies.

00:11:23.382 --> 00:11:23.883
Why?

00:11:23.883 --> 00:11:26.619
Because it's the one S electron.

00:11:26.619 --> 00:11:30.322
And we know that once you
get down to those metals,

00:11:30.322 --> 00:11:32.290
those one s electrons,
you go further

00:11:32.290 --> 00:11:35.294
down the periodic table,
1s electrons are seriously

00:11:35.294 --> 00:11:37.997
tightly bound.

00:11:37.997 --> 00:11:41.834
And those levels have
a difference down there

00:11:41.834 --> 00:11:43.069
that is pretty high in energy.

00:11:43.069 --> 00:11:45.204
It's X-ray high in energy.

00:11:45.204 --> 00:11:46.472
That's the point.

00:11:46.472 --> 00:11:50.242
So now, when that
cascade happens,

00:11:50.242 --> 00:11:53.179
we call that a k alpha.

00:11:53.179 --> 00:11:56.048
And if it were to
have come from here,

00:11:56.048 --> 00:11:57.483
it would be called a k beta.

00:12:01.954 --> 00:12:07.526
And those are transitions, that
unlike this continuous energy,

00:12:07.526 --> 00:12:11.797
those transitions only happen
at very specific energies,

00:12:11.797 --> 00:12:13.265
delta energies.

00:12:13.265 --> 00:12:16.469
The change in
energy from L to K--

00:12:16.469 --> 00:12:20.740
or for K beta it would
be going from M to K.

00:12:20.740 --> 00:12:23.142
It's k alpha, k beta.

00:12:23.142 --> 00:12:26.612
We use the k because that's the
final place the electron goes

00:12:26.612 --> 00:12:29.782
when it decays down.

00:12:29.782 --> 00:12:33.552
So if I just showed
you these, well, that

00:12:33.552 --> 00:12:38.224
would be like k alpha, and
that would be like k beta

00:12:38.224 --> 00:12:42.128
because you know the k beta
is going to be a higher energy

00:12:42.128 --> 00:12:45.998
photon because it came
up from a higher level.

00:12:45.998 --> 00:12:49.935
Well, you would also have
on here some other peaks.

00:12:49.935 --> 00:12:53.439
You would have the L peaks.

00:12:53.439 --> 00:13:02.748
So you'd have like
L alpha, L beta.

00:13:02.748 --> 00:13:06.118
So as you crank the
energy up, then you

00:13:06.118 --> 00:13:08.220
can knock out those
core electrons

00:13:08.220 --> 00:13:11.624
and these cascades happen and
you get these discrete peaks.

00:13:11.624 --> 00:13:16.629
Notice, they will only come
when you have enough energy

00:13:16.629 --> 00:13:20.199
in that incident electron
to knock this electron out

00:13:20.199 --> 00:13:21.534
from the core.

00:13:21.534 --> 00:13:23.769
So that's why they
don't appear until you

00:13:23.769 --> 00:13:26.505
get to a certain
incident electron energy.

00:13:26.505 --> 00:13:29.775
They don't appear until you
get to that certain energy.

00:13:29.775 --> 00:13:35.915
And so we have a video on that,
which also I will narrate.

00:13:39.351 --> 00:13:40.419
Is this it?

00:13:40.419 --> 00:13:42.221
There we go.

00:13:42.221 --> 00:13:43.222
So there it is.

00:13:43.222 --> 00:13:44.123
It's a metal atom.

00:13:44.123 --> 00:13:47.459
I don't know which one.

00:13:47.459 --> 00:13:48.994
OK, there's the
incident electron.

00:13:48.994 --> 00:13:49.695
You fired it.

00:13:49.695 --> 00:13:50.429
And look at that.

00:13:50.429 --> 00:13:52.665
It knocked out a
core 1s electron,

00:13:52.665 --> 00:13:55.301
because it had enough
energy, very high energy.

00:13:55.301 --> 00:13:56.869
That's what Roentgen
did, cranked up

00:13:56.869 --> 00:13:58.571
the voltage, higher keV.

00:13:58.571 --> 00:13:59.338
And there it is.

00:13:59.338 --> 00:14:01.974
A cascade down and
an X-ray comes out.

00:14:01.974 --> 00:14:02.508
You see that?

00:14:06.679 --> 00:14:07.513
Is that all?

00:14:07.513 --> 00:14:09.181
Oh, yeah, and then
it's going to draw--

00:14:09.181 --> 00:14:11.717
because of those, you get
these characteristic peaks.

00:14:11.717 --> 00:14:13.752
Now, we call them
characteristic,

00:14:13.752 --> 00:14:15.321
because now you see why.

00:14:15.321 --> 00:14:19.291
So unlike the
continuous radiation,

00:14:19.291 --> 00:14:23.162
these peaks depend on
the atom, because they

00:14:23.162 --> 00:14:26.198
depend on the energy
levels of the atom.

00:14:26.198 --> 00:14:28.434
And so that's why like
if you looked this up,

00:14:28.434 --> 00:14:31.337
you say, well, OK, let's look
at the k alpha radiation.

00:14:31.337 --> 00:14:33.639
Let's look at the
k alpha peaks that

00:14:33.639 --> 00:14:36.141
come out of different atoms.

00:14:36.141 --> 00:14:37.409
They're going to be different.

00:14:39.945 --> 00:14:44.783
So you have very
sharp lines of x-rays

00:14:44.783 --> 00:14:47.019
at very specific energies.

00:14:47.019 --> 00:14:49.488
For copper, it's 8 keV.

00:14:49.488 --> 00:14:51.857
For molybdenum it's 17.5.

00:14:51.857 --> 00:14:53.626
Silver tungsten, it changes.

00:14:53.626 --> 00:14:59.465
And you can see that it goes
up, as that 1s electron is

00:14:59.465 --> 00:15:02.034
lower and lower energy because
I'm adding all these protons.

00:15:02.034 --> 00:15:05.671
So it all makes sense from
the concepts we've learned.

00:15:05.671 --> 00:15:09.008
Oh but see, now
that's really useful.

00:15:09.008 --> 00:15:11.677
That is really useful,
because now I've

00:15:11.677 --> 00:15:15.447
got a way to have
a source of X-rays

00:15:15.447 --> 00:15:17.616
that is super well defined.

00:15:17.616 --> 00:15:18.317
It's super clear.

00:15:18.317 --> 00:15:21.186
It's always this--
that's so cool.

00:15:21.186 --> 00:15:25.190
As long as I have the same
metal, it's always the same.

00:15:25.190 --> 00:15:26.992
I can increase or decrease--

00:15:26.992 --> 00:15:28.527
well, I can't go
below the threshold.

00:15:28.527 --> 00:15:29.995
But I can't go above it.

00:15:29.995 --> 00:15:34.066
And that peak is
characteristic of the metal.

00:15:34.066 --> 00:15:35.367
So it doesn't change.

00:15:35.367 --> 00:15:39.605
That's really useful because
I've got now a flashlight.

00:15:39.605 --> 00:15:42.241
I've got an X-ray
flashlight where

00:15:42.241 --> 00:15:44.543
the energy that I'm
sending out is always

00:15:44.543 --> 00:15:46.478
exactly what I know it to be.

00:15:46.478 --> 00:15:47.613
I can predict what it is.

00:15:47.613 --> 00:15:50.582
And it always will be that
depending on which medal

00:15:50.582 --> 00:15:52.084
I put in there.

00:15:52.084 --> 00:15:55.621
So that's a useful thing.

00:15:55.621 --> 00:15:57.456
Why is that useful?

00:15:57.456 --> 00:16:01.527
Well, that gets to the topic
that is the topic of today

00:16:01.527 --> 00:16:05.497
and of Friday, which is what
are we doing with these X-rays?

00:16:05.497 --> 00:16:06.999
Well, first, we're
generating them.

00:16:06.999 --> 00:16:09.635
So that's what we've
talked about so far.

00:16:09.635 --> 00:16:12.571
But now, we're going
to actually use

00:16:12.571 --> 00:16:16.375
them to determine the
crystal that we have.

00:16:16.375 --> 00:16:17.743
We're going to
actually use them.

00:16:17.743 --> 00:16:20.913
We're going to use
that flashlight.

00:16:20.913 --> 00:16:23.649
And so you can see why
this would be useful,

00:16:23.649 --> 00:16:26.385
because this is the range--

00:16:26.385 --> 00:16:28.120
we've showed this before--

00:16:28.120 --> 00:16:29.088
of X-rays.

00:16:29.088 --> 00:16:31.290
So they have these
energies of keV.

00:16:31.290 --> 00:16:34.693
And they have wavelengths
right around a few Angstrom.

00:16:34.693 --> 00:16:37.563
That is a little less than an
Angstrom, maybe 2 Angstroms.

00:16:37.563 --> 00:16:39.131
See where those wavelengths are?

00:16:39.131 --> 00:16:42.167
Well, those are atomic spacings.

00:16:42.167 --> 00:16:46.972
Those are like distances
between layers.

00:16:46.972 --> 00:16:49.675
And so if we could
shine these on a crystal

00:16:49.675 --> 00:16:53.645
and somehow figure out what
it is with that light--

00:16:53.645 --> 00:16:55.547
oh, there's a way
we can do that.

00:16:55.547 --> 00:16:57.983
It's called diffraction.

00:16:57.983 --> 00:16:59.952
Because what happens is--

00:16:59.952 --> 00:17:02.287
and we know this
from many fields.

00:17:02.287 --> 00:17:06.791
You can think about this just
as a water wave, any way.

00:17:06.791 --> 00:17:13.665
If the wavelength is similar
in size to the features,

00:17:13.665 --> 00:17:17.603
then you get constructive
and destructive interference

00:17:17.603 --> 00:17:20.472
as a result of the
interaction between the wave

00:17:20.472 --> 00:17:22.207
and the features.

00:17:22.207 --> 00:17:24.910
So that's called diffraction.

00:17:24.910 --> 00:17:27.846
And you can see it here with
this very simple picture

00:17:27.846 --> 00:17:29.648
of say a water-- this
could be like a water

00:17:29.648 --> 00:17:30.849
wave, a sound wave.

00:17:30.849 --> 00:17:31.750
And there it is.

00:17:31.750 --> 00:17:34.887
And it's interfering
both constructively

00:17:34.887 --> 00:17:39.491
and destructively
along these lines.

00:17:39.491 --> 00:17:41.360
You can do this test yourself.

00:17:41.360 --> 00:17:43.328
You can take--

00:17:43.328 --> 00:17:45.564
I highly recommend this--
take a laser pointer.

00:17:49.501 --> 00:17:51.437
Now if I just had
a piece of metal--

00:17:51.437 --> 00:17:54.640
I don't have a piece
of metal, but if I did,

00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:56.341
then I would shine it on it.

00:17:56.341 --> 00:17:58.777
And what you'd see is
that the dot would just

00:17:58.777 --> 00:18:01.213
reflect off the piece of metal.

00:18:01.213 --> 00:18:03.582
So I just would
get the dot back.

00:18:03.582 --> 00:18:06.618
But now I've got
this thing here.

00:18:06.618 --> 00:18:09.488
Many of you may
not know what this.

00:18:09.488 --> 00:18:10.756
This is called a CD.

00:18:13.859 --> 00:18:17.596
But it turns out that a
CD has features in it.

00:18:17.596 --> 00:18:21.934
It's got trenches that are like
a little less than a micron

00:18:21.934 --> 00:18:24.069
apart, 100 nanometers.

00:18:24.069 --> 00:18:26.438
And this is 500 nanometer light.

00:18:26.438 --> 00:18:28.974
So you would expect
there to be diffraction.

00:18:28.974 --> 00:18:32.010
You would expect there to be
constructive and destructive

00:18:32.010 --> 00:18:32.744
interference.

00:18:32.744 --> 00:18:35.180
And when I bounce it off
of this, look at that.

00:18:35.180 --> 00:18:36.582
There it is.

00:18:36.582 --> 00:18:39.351
This never gets old.

00:18:39.351 --> 00:18:43.889
I'm not getting just
one reflection here.

00:18:43.889 --> 00:18:47.426
I'm getting a whole scatter of
them that have constructively

00:18:47.426 --> 00:18:50.229
interfered, because of
the feature sizes being

00:18:50.229 --> 00:18:54.233
the same as the wavelength.

00:18:54.233 --> 00:18:56.301
But now, I want to
do that with X-rays.

00:18:56.301 --> 00:19:00.305
And I want to do
it onto crystals.

00:19:00.305 --> 00:19:02.274
So how do we do that?

00:19:02.274 --> 00:19:03.742
So let's think about that.

00:19:03.742 --> 00:19:05.811
And we're going to think
about it in terms of what

00:19:05.811 --> 00:19:09.148
the Bragg father-son pair, who
won the Nobel Prize and are

00:19:09.148 --> 00:19:10.149
on a stamp.

00:19:10.149 --> 00:19:14.419
That's what you get when you
win a Nobel Prize I guess.

00:19:14.419 --> 00:19:18.857
And what they did is they
figured out how to do this.

00:19:18.857 --> 00:19:22.127
So let's go through that just so
we understand it conceptually.

00:19:26.064 --> 00:19:32.371
So I'm going to say that
I have a set of atoms.

00:19:32.371 --> 00:19:37.276
Now, I'm not going to
worry about what they are.

00:19:37.276 --> 00:19:40.345
But I'm just going to say that
there's some plane of atoms

00:19:40.345 --> 00:19:40.979
here.

00:19:40.979 --> 00:19:44.917
And there's another plane
of atoms beneath that.

00:19:44.917 --> 00:19:48.720
So there is another one
and so on and so on.

00:19:48.720 --> 00:19:53.358
And now, these would
be Miller planes.

00:19:53.358 --> 00:19:55.327
These would be Miller
planes in the crystal.

00:19:55.327 --> 00:19:57.729
And let's just assume
they're very, very simple,

00:19:57.729 --> 00:19:58.964
this plane and that plane.

00:19:58.964 --> 00:20:01.733
And now what I'm going to do is
I'm going to have some x-rays.

00:20:01.733 --> 00:20:05.470
I'm going to have some
light shining on this.

00:20:05.470 --> 00:20:07.873
And it's going to be incident.

00:20:07.873 --> 00:20:09.908
And it's going to be reflected.

00:20:09.908 --> 00:20:14.179
But see, I'm going to
have another wave here.

00:20:14.179 --> 00:20:18.116
OK, let's see if I can
get through this drawing,

00:20:18.116 --> 00:20:22.054
sort of, almost, kind of.

00:20:22.054 --> 00:20:26.258
Now, here's the deal,
these are waves.

00:20:29.328 --> 00:20:32.297
So if I want to
draw this as a wave,

00:20:32.297 --> 00:20:33.599
I might draw it like that.

00:20:33.599 --> 00:20:39.905
And if these waves are
constructively interfering,

00:20:39.905 --> 00:20:46.545
let's just complete that, then
this wave would look like that.

00:20:46.545 --> 00:20:49.381
If they're in phase, then that's
what they would look like.

00:20:49.381 --> 00:20:52.117
Now, this one's getting
reflected off the surface.

00:20:52.117 --> 00:20:53.852
So I'm going to do
that reflection here.

00:20:56.622 --> 00:21:00.192
And if I wanted to come
back off of the surface,

00:21:00.192 --> 00:21:03.295
and this one got
through the first layer,

00:21:03.295 --> 00:21:04.396
this didn't get reflected.

00:21:04.396 --> 00:21:05.230
It's going down.

00:21:05.230 --> 00:21:06.999
And if I want it
to come back up,

00:21:06.999 --> 00:21:11.203
then this one must also look
like that to be in phase.

00:21:11.203 --> 00:21:13.739
They must be in
phases they come out,

00:21:13.739 --> 00:21:16.475
or else they're not going
to interfere constructively.

00:21:16.475 --> 00:21:17.175
You see that?

00:21:17.175 --> 00:21:18.577
So those are my X-rays.

00:21:18.577 --> 00:21:19.578
They're waves.

00:21:19.578 --> 00:21:21.713
They're just waves.

00:21:21.713 --> 00:21:25.017
Oh, but this is
the whole secret,

00:21:25.017 --> 00:21:33.725
because if this angle here is
theta, then what that means

00:21:33.725 --> 00:21:35.027
is that this angle is theta.

00:21:38.030 --> 00:21:41.433
And if that's true,
then this distance

00:21:41.433 --> 00:21:47.339
is d sine theta,
where this is d.

00:21:47.339 --> 00:21:51.843
That's just some
simple geometry.

00:21:51.843 --> 00:21:55.347
So what you know then
is, OK, now we're

00:21:55.347 --> 00:21:59.818
getting somewhere, because
you know if I had a wave,

00:21:59.818 --> 00:22:03.288
this is one wavelength.

00:22:03.288 --> 00:22:07.459
So if I had a wave come
in like this and one

00:22:07.459 --> 00:22:09.594
of them is going to get
reflected off of this lower

00:22:09.594 --> 00:22:12.230
surface and the other one
got reflected on this,

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:14.666
but I don't want them
to interfere in any way

00:22:14.666 --> 00:22:20.105
but constructively, that's what
Bragg said, Bragg and Bragg.

00:22:20.105 --> 00:22:21.139
That's what they said.

00:22:21.139 --> 00:22:23.675
Then the only way
for that to happen

00:22:23.675 --> 00:22:26.878
is if this distance
plus that distance--

00:22:26.878 --> 00:22:29.748
so d sine theta
plus d sine theta

00:22:29.748 --> 00:22:32.651
is equal to some multiple
of the wavelength.

00:22:32.651 --> 00:22:34.653
It has to be.

00:22:34.653 --> 00:22:38.657
And so what you get is-- well,
that's what they have there,

00:22:38.657 --> 00:22:40.692
n lambda, some multiple
of the wavelength,

00:22:40.692 --> 00:22:46.398
equals 2d sine theta,
where theta is the incident

00:22:46.398 --> 00:22:49.968
angle of that X-ray.

00:22:49.968 --> 00:22:51.937
So this is incident--

00:22:51.937 --> 00:22:55.774
just to be clear--
incident X-ray.

00:22:55.774 --> 00:23:01.380
And these are reflected X-rays.

00:23:04.883 --> 00:23:07.419
Now to keep it
simple in this class,

00:23:07.419 --> 00:23:16.194
we're just going to say n
equals 1 for 3.091, just

00:23:16.194 --> 00:23:19.931
to keep things simple,
because I want to grasp

00:23:19.931 --> 00:23:22.567
the basic concepts here.

00:23:22.567 --> 00:23:25.470
We're not using X-rays to
get the structure of DNA.

00:23:25.470 --> 00:23:28.340
But we're going to use X-rays
to figure out cubic crystal

00:23:28.340 --> 00:23:29.408
structures.

00:23:29.408 --> 00:23:30.942
I'm going to show
how that works.

00:23:33.779 --> 00:23:38.049
So this must be true for--

00:23:38.049 --> 00:23:39.985
so this is in parentheses--

00:23:39.985 --> 00:23:42.554
for constructive interference.

00:23:48.293 --> 00:23:51.363
Those dots that
you saw interface--

00:23:51.363 --> 00:23:55.867
interface, no
interference, interference.

00:23:59.671 --> 00:24:02.140
This is true for
constructive interference.

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:04.176
Of course, you could write
any equation you want.

00:24:04.176 --> 00:24:07.012
But if you want them to be
constructively interfering

00:24:07.012 --> 00:24:08.847
when they come out,
that has to be true.

00:24:08.847 --> 00:24:10.182
And that's what the Braggs said.

00:24:10.182 --> 00:24:13.051
But we're not there yet
right, because now we've

00:24:13.051 --> 00:24:15.821
got to do experiments.

00:24:15.821 --> 00:24:17.122
So we've got to do experiments.

00:24:17.122 --> 00:24:19.558
So what does this mean?

00:24:19.558 --> 00:24:28.733
Well, OK, I'm going to
take X-rays of some lambda.

00:24:28.733 --> 00:24:33.572
And I'm going to shine
them on a sample.

00:24:33.572 --> 00:24:35.474
And I'm going to measure.

00:24:35.474 --> 00:24:37.108
So what I'm going to do--

00:24:37.108 --> 00:24:38.643
well, I think I have a picture--

00:24:38.643 --> 00:24:39.811
I'm going to measure--

00:24:39.811 --> 00:24:40.745
there it is.

00:24:40.745 --> 00:24:45.417
This is what an X-ray
diffraction experiment

00:24:45.417 --> 00:24:47.052
looks like.

00:24:47.052 --> 00:24:49.387
So I've got some sample.

00:24:49.387 --> 00:24:50.856
And I shine x-rays.

00:24:50.856 --> 00:24:52.023
I've got a source of x-rays.

00:24:52.023 --> 00:24:56.161
We now know how to
make that source.

00:24:56.161 --> 00:25:00.465
And we'll just filter
out one of these lines.

00:25:00.465 --> 00:25:04.236
So I've got that source,
and I can change the angle.

00:25:04.236 --> 00:25:06.171
And then I've got a detector.

00:25:06.171 --> 00:25:09.241
And I can measure did I
get interference or not?

00:25:09.241 --> 00:25:11.843
It's just like the dots.

00:25:11.843 --> 00:25:17.582
So now, we know that if I
do that and I say I scan--

00:25:22.320 --> 00:25:24.589
so I've got intensity.

00:25:24.589 --> 00:25:27.792
And now I'm plotting
it with angle.

00:25:27.792 --> 00:25:32.697
So now I'm moving the angle
around and I'm changing it.

00:25:32.697 --> 00:25:34.699
And, bam, I get interference.

00:25:34.699 --> 00:25:36.768
And I see a spot in my detector.

00:25:36.768 --> 00:25:39.471
So literally it
would look like this.

00:25:39.471 --> 00:25:42.841
You would get some angle
where there's interference.

00:25:42.841 --> 00:25:45.176
And the detector would say
ding, ding, ding, ding, ding,

00:25:45.176 --> 00:25:48.146
I see a lot of
x-rays coming off.

00:25:48.146 --> 00:25:49.981
And now you change the
angle a few degrees

00:25:49.981 --> 00:25:52.918
and I don't see anything,
because it's all

00:25:52.918 --> 00:25:55.754
destructive from
these crystal planes.

00:25:58.924 --> 00:26:04.763
So it seems like then if I just
vary theta, am I there yet?

00:26:04.763 --> 00:26:10.201
The problem is that
I might not know d.

00:26:10.201 --> 00:26:13.438
A, ha, but we do know d.

00:26:13.438 --> 00:26:16.641
We do know d, because
we learned about d.

00:26:16.641 --> 00:26:19.711
For cubic crystals, we know d.

00:26:19.711 --> 00:26:23.148
Because for cubic
systems, we know

00:26:23.148 --> 00:26:30.221
that d of hkl of any plane, the
distance between those planes

00:26:30.221 --> 00:26:35.126
is equal to a over the
square root of h squared

00:26:35.126 --> 00:26:37.629
plus k squared plus l squared.

00:26:37.629 --> 00:26:39.230
And that's something
that we learned.

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:51.276
This is distance between
Miller planes in cubic crystal.

00:26:54.346 --> 00:26:55.146
I'm saving time.

00:26:55.146 --> 00:26:57.148
I wrote xtal.

00:26:57.148 --> 00:26:59.484
I saved a lot of
time, which I just

00:26:59.484 --> 00:27:01.453
wasted by being so proud of it.

00:27:05.557 --> 00:27:06.157
I know d.

00:27:06.157 --> 00:27:07.792
Well, OK, so what
does that mean?

00:27:07.792 --> 00:27:10.562
Well, let's take a
look, because now I'm

00:27:10.562 --> 00:27:16.234
going to go back my equation.

00:27:16.234 --> 00:27:19.537
And I'm going to say,
OK, lambda equals 2d--

00:27:19.537 --> 00:27:21.906
and I'm saying n equals 1.

00:27:21.906 --> 00:27:23.475
And I'm not going
to be very specific.

00:27:23.475 --> 00:27:28.013
This is now a d that comes
from the spacing between planes

00:27:28.013 --> 00:27:30.849
that are specified
by the Miller indices

00:27:30.849 --> 00:27:35.220
times the sine of theta.

00:27:35.220 --> 00:27:40.158
And I'm putting hkl on the theta
as well, and you'll see why.

00:27:40.158 --> 00:27:45.163
Because here's the thing,
now I've got constants.

00:27:45.163 --> 00:27:47.365
Now I've got constants,
because look,

00:27:47.365 --> 00:27:53.972
this is fixed by the source.

00:27:53.972 --> 00:27:56.508
So this is a constant.

00:27:56.508 --> 00:28:00.945
If I have copper, then
it's 1.54 Angstroms.

00:28:00.945 --> 00:28:02.781
This is a constant.

00:28:02.781 --> 00:28:05.483
This is fixed by the crystal.

00:28:05.483 --> 00:28:07.619
That's also a constant.

00:28:07.619 --> 00:28:13.358
Fixed by the crystal,
because that's also

00:28:13.358 --> 00:28:15.326
a constants, because it's
the lattice constant.

00:28:15.326 --> 00:28:16.961
We're not changing that.

00:28:16.961 --> 00:28:20.732
So for a given set of
planes that these waves

00:28:20.732 --> 00:28:26.271
are bouncing off of and maybe
constructively interfering

00:28:26.271 --> 00:28:31.109
with, that depending
on the theta,

00:28:31.109 --> 00:28:33.411
then these are constants.

00:28:36.081 --> 00:28:39.684
So if I regroup then-- so
I'm going to regroup them.

00:28:39.684 --> 00:28:42.353
And so I'm going to say that--

00:28:42.353 --> 00:28:47.225
let's see, d-- so I'm going to
substitute in that expression

00:28:47.225 --> 00:28:50.495
up there down here.

00:28:50.495 --> 00:28:52.931
And I'm going to
use a copper source.

00:28:52.931 --> 00:28:55.366
So I'm going to say-- well,
let me go through this one step

00:28:55.366 --> 00:28:55.867
at a time.

00:28:55.867 --> 00:29:01.372
So d equals 1.54
Angstroms over--

00:29:04.209 --> 00:29:10.148
let's see-- 2 sine theta hkl.

00:29:10.148 --> 00:29:11.816
Now what have I done here?

00:29:11.816 --> 00:29:15.453
This is for a copper source.

00:29:15.453 --> 00:29:20.992
So I'm fixing in the constant.

00:29:20.992 --> 00:29:22.260
So that's the Bragg condition.

00:29:22.260 --> 00:29:26.898
But I also know
that d is equal to--

00:29:29.467 --> 00:29:30.869
I don't want to write it again.

00:29:30.869 --> 00:29:33.471
It's also equal to that.

00:29:33.471 --> 00:29:35.173
OK, good.

00:29:35.173 --> 00:29:37.008
OK, so let's put that together.

00:29:37.008 --> 00:29:38.576
We'll do a little division.

00:29:38.576 --> 00:29:48.119
And what we get is 1.54
Angstroms over 2a squared,

00:29:48.119 --> 00:29:49.120
the whole thing squared.

00:29:49.120 --> 00:29:51.456
I'm squaring it-- I don't
want the square root--

00:29:51.456 --> 00:29:58.029
equals sine squared
theta hkl over h

00:29:58.029 --> 00:30:02.500
squared plus k squared
plus l squared for--

00:30:02.500 --> 00:30:14.078
let's be very specific-- for
constructive interference

00:30:14.078 --> 00:30:16.681
and a copper source.

00:30:16.681 --> 00:30:18.483
So now, I'm getting
specific because this

00:30:18.483 --> 00:30:20.852
is how experiments with
X-ray diffraction are done.

00:30:24.389 --> 00:30:27.659
But now the last time
I checked, if you

00:30:27.659 --> 00:30:31.996
got something equal
to a constant,

00:30:31.996 --> 00:30:35.033
then that something
also is a constant.

00:30:35.033 --> 00:30:37.902
And this is a constant.

00:30:37.902 --> 00:30:42.240
And so what I need to
do now is figure out I'm

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:47.045
going to measure these data
is where I get a signal.

00:30:47.045 --> 00:30:49.547
Remember, I'm going
to now change theta so

00:30:49.547 --> 00:30:52.617
that I see where I get signals.

00:30:52.617 --> 00:31:00.024
Now, those thetas
divided by the hkl

00:31:00.024 --> 00:31:03.428
that they are bouncing
off of must be a constant.

00:31:03.428 --> 00:31:04.929
They must not change.

00:31:04.929 --> 00:31:08.132
That is at the heart
of X-ray diffraction.

00:31:08.132 --> 00:31:11.035
That is at the heart of it.

00:31:11.035 --> 00:31:16.074
And so we're going to do
that with a specific example

00:31:16.074 --> 00:31:17.242
today and Friday.

00:31:17.242 --> 00:31:19.611
But before we do that,
there's another thing

00:31:19.611 --> 00:31:20.445
that we can observe.

00:31:20.445 --> 00:31:22.213
There's another thing
that we can observe.

00:31:22.213 --> 00:31:23.848
Oh, this is what
it would look like.

00:31:23.848 --> 00:31:26.117
So here it is.

00:31:26.117 --> 00:31:29.020
So now I I've changed--
now, why do we do 2 theta?

00:31:29.020 --> 00:31:30.455
It's kind of historical.

00:31:30.455 --> 00:31:34.626
You plot X-ray
diffraction spectra.

00:31:34.626 --> 00:31:37.862
So this is an XRD, X-ray
diffraction spectrum.

00:31:37.862 --> 00:31:40.531
This is the intensity
of the peaks.

00:31:40.531 --> 00:31:42.333
And these are the peaks.

00:31:42.333 --> 00:31:43.434
This is a beautiful thing.

00:31:43.434 --> 00:31:45.603
I'm seeing a crystal here.

00:31:45.603 --> 00:31:46.571
I'm seeing a crystal.

00:31:46.571 --> 00:31:49.707
And by Friday, you will
be seeing a crystal.

00:31:49.707 --> 00:31:50.708
Those aren't just peaks.

00:31:50.708 --> 00:31:53.444
Those are planes in a crystal.

00:31:53.444 --> 00:31:55.980
Those are planes in a
crystal, which tells me

00:31:55.980 --> 00:31:58.082
not only that I
have these planes,

00:31:58.082 --> 00:31:59.984
but it tells me
what crystal I have.

00:31:59.984 --> 00:32:02.120
But that's not how we start.

00:32:02.120 --> 00:32:04.289
The way we start is we
do these measurements

00:32:04.289 --> 00:32:05.857
and we just read off angles.

00:32:05.857 --> 00:32:09.193
So we got to get from there to
there, to crystal structure.

00:32:09.193 --> 00:32:12.697
So what I want to determine
is the crystal structure

00:32:12.697 --> 00:32:13.731
and the lattice constant.

00:32:13.731 --> 00:32:15.033
That's my goal.

00:32:15.033 --> 00:32:17.001
What I have is a
spectrum that looks

00:32:17.001 --> 00:32:18.503
like this where
all I've done here

00:32:18.503 --> 00:32:22.006
is put these
specific angles here.

00:32:22.006 --> 00:32:25.009
And you have to be given--

00:32:25.009 --> 00:32:27.979
so this is the
aluminum XRD spectrum.

00:32:27.979 --> 00:32:31.282
So if you shine
X-rays on aluminum,

00:32:31.282 --> 00:32:34.419
this is what you
get if you know also

00:32:34.419 --> 00:32:37.188
that those X-rays are
from copper, which

00:32:37.188 --> 00:32:39.958
means that lambda is fixed.

00:32:39.958 --> 00:32:44.462
So this should be like the
information you get to start.

00:32:44.462 --> 00:32:47.098
You'd be given this
spectrum, given these peaks.

00:32:47.098 --> 00:32:48.900
And you've be giving
this information here.

00:32:48.900 --> 00:32:50.401
It's a copper target.

00:32:50.401 --> 00:32:52.637
And from that, we can
determine the crystal structure

00:32:52.637 --> 00:32:53.638
in the lattice constant.

00:32:53.638 --> 00:32:59.911
Now, there's something--
oh, why do we do 2 theta?

00:32:59.911 --> 00:33:01.312
Well, it's historical.

00:33:01.312 --> 00:33:04.882
It could have been theta to
make all those dividing by 2s

00:33:04.882 --> 00:33:05.350
go away.

00:33:05.350 --> 00:33:09.020
But instead, you can see
that as I rotate this,

00:33:09.020 --> 00:33:10.788
this changes by theta.

00:33:10.788 --> 00:33:12.957
The detector changes by 2 theta.

00:33:12.957 --> 00:33:16.561
So that's why extra spectra
are given in 2 theta.

00:33:16.561 --> 00:33:19.964
There's no other real
good reason for it,

00:33:19.964 --> 00:33:22.600
even though in the Bragg
condition, it's not 2 theta,

00:33:22.600 --> 00:33:23.601
it's theta.

00:33:23.601 --> 00:33:27.205
This comes from
geometry of the planes.

00:33:27.205 --> 00:33:31.275
And this just comes
from historical setups

00:33:31.275 --> 00:33:34.045
and how you move the detector.

00:33:34.045 --> 00:33:38.282
So what's measured and
plotted is the 2 theta.

00:33:38.282 --> 00:33:41.319
But before we go, before
we do this transformation,

00:33:41.319 --> 00:33:43.654
where we take an X-ray
spectrum like this

00:33:43.654 --> 00:33:47.158
and we get the information we
want, there's one more thing.

00:33:47.158 --> 00:33:51.829
And that is not all
reflections are allowed.

00:33:51.829 --> 00:33:53.698
Not all reflections are allowed.

00:33:53.698 --> 00:33:57.301
And so let's talk about
that, and then we'll

00:33:57.301 --> 00:34:00.071
come back to the spectrum.

00:34:00.071 --> 00:34:03.074
Now, you can kind
of understand this

00:34:03.074 --> 00:34:07.578
by looking at just a simple
kind of comparison here.

00:34:10.380 --> 00:34:11.482
So these are the hkl's.

00:34:11.482 --> 00:34:14.786
Remember, that's the
hkl for a Miller plane.

00:34:14.786 --> 00:34:17.021
This is h squared plus k
squared plus l squared.

00:34:17.021 --> 00:34:18.356
Why do we put that?

00:34:18.356 --> 00:34:20.091
Because we know we're
going to need it.

00:34:20.091 --> 00:34:21.492
There it is right there.

00:34:21.492 --> 00:34:24.562
So we know we're
going to need it.

00:34:24.562 --> 00:34:29.199
But if you look at the simple
cubic, that's simple cubic.

00:34:29.199 --> 00:34:32.670
Any combination of hkl is OK.

00:34:32.670 --> 00:34:38.042
There is no combination that
would give you interference

00:34:38.042 --> 00:34:41.245
along those plains stacking.

00:34:41.245 --> 00:34:44.581
You may say, well, OK, yeah,
what are you talking about?

00:34:44.581 --> 00:34:45.983
Why are you even
bringing this up?

00:34:45.983 --> 00:34:48.485
Well, when you see the
other two crystal structures

00:34:48.485 --> 00:34:50.321
you'll see what I mean.

00:34:50.321 --> 00:34:53.558
So now we have the case of BCC--

00:34:53.558 --> 00:34:56.527
when you see this,
you see BCC and FCC.

00:34:56.527 --> 00:34:59.764
And what I'm showing you
here isn't the 100 plane.

00:34:59.764 --> 00:35:01.599
It's the 200 plane.

00:35:01.599 --> 00:35:02.633
So this is the family--

00:35:02.633 --> 00:35:04.435
remember, the family--
of 200 planes.

00:35:04.435 --> 00:35:05.069
There they are.

00:35:08.206 --> 00:35:12.877
But now you see
that what happens--

00:35:12.877 --> 00:35:14.479
and I have a picture
here to show you,

00:35:14.479 --> 00:35:15.513
but I'll tell you first--

00:35:15.513 --> 00:35:18.182
what happens is
the light comes in.

00:35:18.182 --> 00:35:20.618
So there's those
squiggly X-rays.

00:35:20.618 --> 00:35:21.519
It comes in.

00:35:21.519 --> 00:35:24.288
And there is, OK,
d, which depends

00:35:24.288 --> 00:35:26.023
on the lattice
constant, is related

00:35:26.023 --> 00:35:27.024
to the lattice constant.

00:35:27.024 --> 00:35:27.625
But look.

00:35:27.625 --> 00:35:30.261
Now there's another
plane in between.

00:35:30.261 --> 00:35:31.629
There's another
plane in between.

00:35:31.629 --> 00:35:33.698
And in fact, with
the 200 planes,

00:35:33.698 --> 00:35:36.367
that plane in between
exactly cancels out

00:35:36.367 --> 00:35:37.602
the constructive interference.

00:35:37.602 --> 00:35:39.070
Here it is.

00:35:39.070 --> 00:35:42.907
So there's what
I would have had.

00:35:42.907 --> 00:35:48.246
If you want to think about this
as the 100, there's the 200s.

00:35:48.246 --> 00:35:53.784
But notice when I go from 100 to
200s, I add this plane in here.

00:35:53.784 --> 00:35:57.221
And because that
plane has atoms in it,

00:35:57.221 --> 00:36:00.725
because that plane has atoms
in it, it acts like a mirror,

00:36:00.725 --> 00:36:04.295
and it can also reflect.

00:36:04.295 --> 00:36:07.665
And so what happens is I would
have had this nice-- there

00:36:07.665 --> 00:36:08.166
it is.

00:36:08.166 --> 00:36:09.200
There's a picture I drew.

00:36:09.200 --> 00:36:11.502
There's that first
X-ray bouncing off.

00:36:11.502 --> 00:36:13.104
There's the second
one bouncing off.

00:36:13.104 --> 00:36:14.672
And those are nicely in phase.

00:36:14.672 --> 00:36:16.307
So I would see that.

00:36:16.307 --> 00:36:18.042
If that's the angle
that gave that to me,

00:36:18.042 --> 00:36:19.443
I'd see that in the detector.

00:36:19.443 --> 00:36:24.348
But now, for BCC or FCC, I've
got something in the middle.

00:36:24.348 --> 00:36:27.952
And that something in the
middle is exactly canceling out.

00:36:27.952 --> 00:36:28.653
You see that?

00:36:28.653 --> 00:36:30.288
So now, it cancels that out.

00:36:30.288 --> 00:36:32.623
In fact, that's
called forbidden.

00:36:32.623 --> 00:36:33.624
You won't see a signal.

00:36:36.694 --> 00:36:40.731
And so these are
called selection rules.

00:36:40.731 --> 00:36:45.903
And for a simple cubic, you
can see there's nothing inside.

00:36:45.903 --> 00:36:49.774
So there's nothing in this
unit cell that could do this.

00:36:49.774 --> 00:36:50.875
So everything's allowed.

00:36:50.875 --> 00:36:53.344
Whether there's a plane in
there or not, it doesn't matter.

00:36:53.344 --> 00:36:57.548
The selection rule is
whether it's ever allowed.

00:36:57.548 --> 00:36:59.483
And for a simple
cubic, everything's

00:36:59.483 --> 00:37:01.886
fine, because nothing
would cancel out.

00:37:01.886 --> 00:37:04.722
But in here, you see
in this 200 case,

00:37:04.722 --> 00:37:06.924
you can see very clearly
from that picture

00:37:06.924 --> 00:37:07.725
how it cancels out.

00:37:07.725 --> 00:37:12.730
But there's many other
kinds of angles or planes

00:37:12.730 --> 00:37:14.999
that might also do that.

00:37:14.999 --> 00:37:16.434
And so I'm going
to just give you

00:37:16.434 --> 00:37:17.635
what the selection rules are.

00:37:17.635 --> 00:37:21.105
We won't go through
and derive them all.

00:37:21.105 --> 00:37:26.577
But let's see, they are
actually quite simple.

00:37:26.577 --> 00:37:28.879
And so I'm going
to write them down.

00:37:28.879 --> 00:37:39.390
So if we look at
allowed reflections,

00:37:39.390 --> 00:37:47.164
and then we look at
forbidden reflections--

00:37:47.164 --> 00:37:50.868
so this is what the
selection rules tell us--

00:37:50.868 --> 00:38:02.313
so if it's simple cubic,
then it's any h, k, and l.

00:38:02.313 --> 00:38:06.217
And there's no
forbidden reflections.

00:38:06.217 --> 00:38:13.624
But if we go through BCC
and FCC, then what we find

00:38:13.624 --> 00:38:16.027
is that for BCC
the selection rule

00:38:16.027 --> 00:38:20.865
is that h plus k
plus l equals even.

00:38:20.865 --> 00:38:24.268
If that is even-- and
we won't derive these,

00:38:24.268 --> 00:38:26.404
but it comes from the
same very simple picture

00:38:26.404 --> 00:38:27.571
I just showed you.

00:38:27.571 --> 00:38:30.174
If something is in there
that can cancel out

00:38:30.174 --> 00:38:34.211
the constructive interference,
it's going to be forbidden.

00:38:34.211 --> 00:38:37.281
Otherwise, it can
constructively interfere.

00:38:37.281 --> 00:38:38.616
And that's what this tells us.

00:38:38.616 --> 00:38:41.385
For BCC, it turns out
to be h plus k plus l.

00:38:41.385 --> 00:38:44.021
And so here, what's
forbidden for BCC

00:38:44.021 --> 00:38:47.158
is h plus k plus l is odd.

00:38:47.158 --> 00:38:57.435
And for FCC it's h, k,
l, all odd or all even.

00:39:00.104 --> 00:39:10.881
And the forbidden FCC is
h, k, l, mixed odd/even.

00:39:10.881 --> 00:39:12.116
These are the selection rules.

00:39:16.921 --> 00:39:23.260
So if I were to give you the
planes that you see, then

00:39:23.260 --> 00:39:26.797
right away from
an X-ray spectrum,

00:39:26.797 --> 00:39:28.933
you could just use
these selection rules

00:39:28.933 --> 00:39:32.837
right away to know something,
to know something about it.

00:39:32.837 --> 00:39:34.572
And if you work this
out and you look at,

00:39:34.572 --> 00:39:37.908
OK, so we have simple cubic.

00:39:37.908 --> 00:39:43.114
So h squared plus k squared
plus 1, 100, BCC, FCC, you're

00:39:43.114 --> 00:39:44.982
not going to see it.

00:39:44.982 --> 00:39:48.552
That doesn't mean that there is
no 100 plane in those crystals.

00:39:48.552 --> 00:39:50.955
It just means that if
you shine X-rays on it,

00:39:50.955 --> 00:39:51.822
you will not see it.

00:39:54.892 --> 00:39:58.329
OK, so the 110,
though, now here, OK,

00:39:58.329 --> 00:40:03.167
so we see our-- by the way,
mixed, even, odd, and it

00:40:03.167 --> 00:40:04.301
adds to odd.

00:40:04.301 --> 00:40:06.237
That's why neither
one of these works.

00:40:06.237 --> 00:40:09.073
Here we go, adds
to even, BCC OK,

00:40:09.073 --> 00:40:13.077
but it's still mixed
even odd, won't be FCC.

00:40:13.077 --> 00:40:20.518
3, it's not mixed, so it can
be a allowable reflection

00:40:20.518 --> 00:40:24.054
for FCC, but not for BBC.

00:40:24.054 --> 00:40:27.158
Because if you add
them up, it's on odd,

00:40:27.158 --> 00:40:28.526
and so forth and so forth.

00:40:28.526 --> 00:40:31.395
And look it, 7 doesn't exist,
because you can't do it.

00:40:31.395 --> 00:40:34.732
No matter how hard you
try, you can't get 7.

00:40:34.732 --> 00:40:35.866
That's OK.

00:40:35.866 --> 00:40:37.701
And there's 8 and
9 and so forth.

00:40:37.701 --> 00:40:41.505
So 9 also-- so
here it's allowed,

00:40:41.505 --> 00:40:43.841
but it's not allowed
in either of these,

00:40:43.841 --> 00:40:53.150
because you can't get either
of these to be satisfied.

00:40:53.150 --> 00:40:55.319
This is what selection
rules give us.

00:40:55.319 --> 00:41:00.858
And it comes,
again, from simple--

00:41:00.858 --> 00:41:02.726
oh, OK, well, that
that's another thing.

00:41:02.726 --> 00:41:05.429
I said a simple, I have
to tell you something,

00:41:05.429 --> 00:41:11.635
because the Bragg condition,
it relies on an assumption

00:41:11.635 --> 00:41:13.604
that's mostly true.

00:41:13.604 --> 00:41:26.350
But the Bragg condition
requires that the reflection

00:41:26.350 --> 00:41:28.419
is independent--

00:41:28.419 --> 00:41:31.922
some of you may be thinking
I did I draw it onto the atom

00:41:31.922 --> 00:41:33.724
or did I draw it
in between atoms?

00:41:33.724 --> 00:41:34.825
Where did that thing--

00:41:34.825 --> 00:41:36.427
does it have to
reflect off an atom?

00:41:36.427 --> 00:41:37.928
We're not going there.

00:41:37.928 --> 00:41:44.869
With the Bragg condition,
it's independent of the atom

00:41:44.869 --> 00:41:46.704
positions in a plane.

00:41:52.843 --> 00:41:54.778
And the second
thing which is what

00:41:54.778 --> 00:41:59.517
I've been sort of alluding
to is that the atomic planes

00:41:59.517 --> 00:42:00.351
are mirror like.

00:42:10.794 --> 00:42:12.897
I mean, this is sort of
an obvious assumption

00:42:12.897 --> 00:42:15.799
since I've been assuming
they've been mirrors.

00:42:15.799 --> 00:42:22.239
But if you start thinking
about atom position,

00:42:22.239 --> 00:42:26.076
you might go back to that
selection rule picture

00:42:26.076 --> 00:42:28.712
and say, well, wait
a second, does it

00:42:28.712 --> 00:42:30.214
always have to hit the atom?

00:42:30.214 --> 00:42:32.550
What if this one was over
or something like that?

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:36.020
No, no, we assume it's
just one continuous plane

00:42:36.020 --> 00:42:40.124
if there are atoms in it.

00:42:40.124 --> 00:42:44.828
If there's no atom in it, then
it's not a reflective plane.

00:42:44.828 --> 00:42:47.398
But it's continuous in the
assumption of the Bragg

00:42:47.398 --> 00:42:48.332
condition.

00:42:52.903 --> 00:42:54.638
Now we go back to our picture.

00:42:54.638 --> 00:43:00.077
So what we want to do
is, again, our goal,

00:43:00.077 --> 00:43:08.385
should we choose, our goal is
to go from this spectrum knowing

00:43:08.385 --> 00:43:09.920
this information--

00:43:09.920 --> 00:43:13.824
it's a copper target and being
able to read off the peak--

00:43:13.824 --> 00:43:16.493
our goal is to determine
the crystal structure

00:43:16.493 --> 00:43:18.062
and the lattice constant.

00:43:18.062 --> 00:43:20.864
That's our goal.

00:43:20.864 --> 00:43:23.300
And let me let me
just write this again,

00:43:23.300 --> 00:43:25.903
because it's
extremely important.

00:43:25.903 --> 00:43:29.540
So maybe I'll keep that one.

00:43:29.540 --> 00:43:32.576
I'm going to erase this and
put it right in the middle,

00:43:32.576 --> 00:43:38.582
because this is what drives XRD.

00:43:38.582 --> 00:43:42.019
This is what drives
XRD, which is

00:43:42.019 --> 00:44:07.978
that our goal is figure out
what makes this constant always.

00:44:07.978 --> 00:44:10.214
Now, you say what is
this constant always?

00:44:12.783 --> 00:44:17.488
This is the expression, which
I'm going to write again.

00:44:17.488 --> 00:44:21.091
So what I have if
it's a copper source

00:44:21.091 --> 00:44:26.330
is 1.54 Angstroms
